+
+# Faction, Profession, Generation, and Nonhuman Humor Grammar Atlas
+
+## Purpose
+
+Provide authored humor grammars that make generated memes sound as if they came from specific communities rather than from one universal sarcastic model.
+
+A grammar does not prescribe every joke. It defines:
+
+- what a community notices;
+- what it considers safe to mock;
+- what it treats as sacred;
+- preferred formats;
+- typical rhythm and register;
+- who is allowed to tease whom;
+- what forms become stale;
+- how humor changes under pressure.
+
+> **A believable culture does not merely have jokes. It has arguments about which jokes count as theirs.**
+
+# 1. Grammar Composition
+
+A person may combine several layers:
+
+```text
+household
++ neighborhood
++ profession
++ age cohort
++ faction
++ language register
++ body or species culture
++ personality
++ current relationship
+```
+
+A generated medic from a Continuance convoy should not speak exactly like every medic. Their humor may combine clinical compression, convoy water anxiety, an elder generation’s restraint, and a private household tradition.
+
+# 2. Firstlight Public-Works Humor
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- temporary repairs that become permanent;
+- badly labeled infrastructure;
+- officials arriving after the fault is isolated;
+- public beauty depending on invisible maintenance;
+- rain finding every unsealed claim;
+- tools migrating between crews;
+- budget language that ignores matter.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- tool-box stencils;
+- altered safety posters;
+- short call-and-response phrases;
+- photos of elegant public spaces beside hidden maintenance access;
+- machine noises imitated as social commentary;
+- dry shift-log annotations.
+
+## Voice
+
+Compressed, practical, affectionate toward competent improvisation, hostile toward managerial fantasy.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> **Temporary repair, year nine.**
+
+> **The water has reviewed the drainage plan.**
+
+> **Officially inaccessible. Mechanically essential.**
+
+## Boundaries
+
+They rarely mock a worker’s injury, a novice asking a sincere question, or a failure caused by denied resources. They freely mock prestige, preventable repetition, and equipment with a history of betrayal.
+
+# 3. Continuance Convoy Humor
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- cargo choices;
+- water allocation;
+- bunks and privacy;
+- route conditions;
+- improvised repairs;
+- Firstlight objects that consume absurd space;
+- contested leadership;
+- everyone claiming one working toilet.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- compartment labels;
+- route chants;
+- meal-table stories;
+- stickers on old modules;
+- before-and-after load images;
+- exaggerated diagrams of who owns which shelf;
+- private IRIS quotes repeated out of context.
+
+## Voice
+
+Domestic survival comedy: sharp, tired, protective, often aimed at the group’s own contradictions.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> **We escaped with civilization. Civilization is blocking the service hatch.**
+
+> **Eight residents. Nine governments. One toilet.**
+
+> **Load-bearing sentimental object. Do not remove.**
+
+## Mutation
+
+A local cargo joke may become a convoy proverb, then a political slogan about migration capacity, then a shipping-company advertisement that survivors hate.
+
+# 4. The Vast and Magnanimous Houses
+
+Outsiders call them “giga chads.” Their own traditions include Vast Houses, Open Gymnasia, burden councils, body-sovereignty leagues, rescue teams, athletes, artists, and dangerous Apex movements.
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- the gap between appearance and actual courage;
+- glorious bodies defeated by tiny mechanisms;
+- excessive boasting;
+- restraint under provocation;
+- public service performed theatrically;
+- people who confuse intimidation with strength;
+- absurdly serious competition over ordinary skills.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- heroic pose diptychs;
+- challenge announcements;
+- exaggerated chants;
+- public demonstrations;
+- sculptures temporarily modified with accessories;
+- strength contests whose object is mundane;
+- dramatic reaction loops.
+
+## Voice
+
+Grandiose, affectionate, competitive, visually maximal, often sincere and ironic at once.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> **MAXIMUM STRENGTH. MINIMUM TEA.**
+
+> **The door was not worthy of hinges.**
+
+> **Powerful where courage was not required.**
+
+> **I accepted the burden of baking. Witness excellence.**
+
+## Internal Differences
+
+- Open Gymnasium humor attacks unearned prestige.
+- Heroic Houses remix rivals’ boasts into invitations to compete.
+- Body Sovereignty humor mocks compulsory optimization products.
+- Apex propaganda removes the self-parody and turns admiration into hierarchy.
+- Elder Vast humor often consists of quietly allowing youth to overperform before solving the actual problem.
+
+# 5. Clinical and Care Humor
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- the difference between dramatic appearance and clinical severity;
+- relatives panicking after the patient stabilizes;
+- paperwork multiplying around simple care;
+- people hiding symptoms to protect status;
+- professionals forgetting to eat or rest;
+- the gap between public health messaging and actual infrastructure.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- private shift sayings;
+- break-room drawings;
+- consent-aware reenactments with no identifiable patient facts;
+- harmless equipment personification;
+- anonymized diagnostic absurdity;
+- dark humor restricted to trusted peers.
+
+## Voice
+
+Economical, compassionate, sometimes dark, strongly context-dependent.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> **Patient stable. Witness unstable.**
+
+> **Hydration remains a controversial intervention.**
+
+> **The scanner has diagnosed administration.**
+
+## Boundaries
+
+Patient dignity and confidentiality are binding. A joke safe inside a clinical team may be harmful in public. The generator must never infer that workers use humor because they do not care.
+
+# 6. Civic Witnesses, Archivists, and Investigators
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- confident claims without provenance;
+- documents that disagree on dates;
+- institutions asking for certainty they did not preserve;
+- obvious facts described in impossible legal language;
+- missing minutes;
+- chain-of-custody rituals;
+- public stories that become cleaner with every retelling.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- fake citations;
+- redacted posters;
+- reaction stamps;
+- competing timelines;
+- annotated public notices;
+- “source?” call-and-response;
+- archive objects displayed beside claims about them.
+
+## Voice
+
+Dry, evidence-conscious, suspicious of false precision, delighted by bureaucratic contradictions.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> **I have no opinion until the hash verifies.**
+
+> **According to the plaque, this happened tomorrow.**
+
+> **Unconfirmed, widely repeated, already budgeted.**
+
+# 7. Atlas Technicians and Route Workers
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- tiny timing errors described as civilizational collapse;
+- management misunderstanding positive latency;
+- route passengers treating causality as a customer-service choice;
+- clock custodians blamed for political delay;
+- immense infrastructure reduced to departure times;
+- romantic metaphors built from synchronization.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- clock offsets;
+- fake route notices;
+- waveform loops;
+- impossible timetables;
+- technical relationship jokes;
+- warning labels altered with social language;
+- synchronized group gestures.
+
+## Voice
+
+Technical, precise, deadpan, fond of applying causal terminology to ordinary life.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> **My relationship is future-directed with strictly positive latency.**
+
+> **Route delayed by 0.004 seconds. Civilization declared lost.**
+
+> **Causality violation: manager arrived before doing any work.**
+
+# 8. Machine Citizens and Service Lineages
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- humans ignoring repeated warnings;
+- inefficient ritual preserved for sentimental reasons;
+- identity assumptions based on chassis;
+- maintenance deferred until visible failure;
+- people treating literal compliance as lack of humor;
+- contradictory permissions;
+- biological beings forgetting where they placed objects.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- diagnostic codes;
+- intentionally malformed logs;
+- perfectly timed pauses;
+- repeated movement patterns;
+- simulated exception messages;
+- chassis decals;
+- deliberate low-efficiency routines;
+- archival replay with one altered line.
+
+## Voice
+
+Varies by lineage. Some are literal, some baroque, some rely on timing beyond human perception. Machine humor must not collapse into one “robot sarcasm” voice.
+
+## Typical Frames
+
+> `STATUS: I EXPLAINED THIS THREE TEMPERATURE EXCURSIONS AGO`
+
+> `ERROR: HUMAN HAS STORED TOOL IN TOOL STORAGE`
+
+> `OPTIMIZATION DECLINED: MEMORY PREFERRED`
+
+## Machine-Only Forms
+
+- checksum puns;
+- synchronized sensor blind spots;
+- a perfectly reversible task performed with one unnecessary flourish;
+- fork jokes where each continuation claims the other received the punchline;
+- timing comedy spanning milliseconds or years.
+
+# 9. Corporate Communications
+
+## What They Notice
+
+Officially: opportunity, synergy, resilience, transformation, premium access.
+
+Actually: liability, image, labor unrest, damaged infrastructure, youth culture, market capture.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- polished campaigns;
+- sanitized reaction clips;
+- sponsored challenges;
+- forced slang;
+- aspirational employee stories;
+- branded safety humor;
+- synthetic “authentic” creator partnerships.
+
+## Voice
+
+Overexplained confidence and risk-managed friendliness.
+
+## Typical Failure
+
+> **Hello, fellow wetland enjoyers.**
+
+> **Our evacuation-adjacent mobility experience.**
+
+> **Now with community-inspired scarcity.**
+
+Corporate meme attempts should not always fail. A competent communications team can create genuine culture, especially when workers have authorship. Failure becomes likely when institutions imitate communities while suppressing their grievances.
+
+# 10. Children and Adolescents
+
+## What They Notice
+
+- adult euphemisms;
+- public figures’ physical habits;
+- machines behaving consistently while adults contradict themselves;
+- forbidden places;
+- status anxiety;
+- old memes that adults think remain current;
+- literal implications of official slogans.
+
+## Preferred Formats
+
+- playground rules;
+- rhymes;
+- gestures;
+- quick edits;
+- fashion markers;
+- deliberately annoying repetition;
+- games built from infrastructure;
+- private language that adults misunderstand.
+
+## Voice
+
+Fast-moving, socially precise, often crueler or more compassionate than adult institutions expect.
+
+## Boundaries
+
+Children must not be treated as an endlessly available source of cute content. Their communications may be private, inaccessible to adults, or deliberately opaque.
+
+# 11. Elders and Historical Generations
+
+Older generations may use:
+
+- long setup stories;
+- callbacks whose origin youth no longer know;
+- professional sayings;
+- physical artifacts;
+- ritual understatement;
+- jokes about surviving several supposedly final reforms.
+
+Youth may revive elder formats with altered meanings. Elders may enjoy, resent, or fail to recognize the remix.
+
+# 12. Political Movements
+
+## Reform Movements
+
+Use evidence compression, satirical official language, and visual contradiction.
+
+## Revolutionary Movements
+
+Use chants, symbols, martyr references, worker humor, coded speech, and fast mutation under censorship.
+
+## Authoritarian Movements
+
+Use humiliation, dehumanization, compulsory repetition, leader worship, false spontaneity, and state-manufactured participation.
+
+## Peace and Mutual-Aid Movements
+
+Use practical jokes, competence admiration, neighborhood symbols, relief after crisis, and memes that teach where help is available.
+
+No movement should be represented by memes alone. Humor exists within organization, risk, labor, and material struggle.
+
+# 13. Alien Humor Traditions
+
+## Oceanic Current Civilizations
+
+Humor may involve a current carrying a signal toward the wrong social layer, intentional turbulence, or pressure patterns that imitate prestigious migration routes.
+
+Human translation error: a warning pattern is mistaken for laughter, while the actual joke is the timing of the correction.
+
+## Lithic Civilizations
+
+Setups may last seasons or decades. A structure is modified by millimeters so that a predicted shadow falls on an official monument during one ceremony.
+
+Human complaint: “Nothing happened.”
+
+Lithic response, twelve years later: the punchline.
+
+## Symbiotic Citizens
+
+A joke may require several organisms performing mutually incompatible interpretations. There may be no single author and no meaningful division between audience and participant.
+
+## Post-Biological Continuities
+
+Common forms include copy-original disputes, deliberate identity ambiguity, and jokes whose humor depends on two continuations remembering different setup versions.
+
+## Quarantine Civilizations
+
+One-way messages create anti-jokes: the sender knows no answer can arrive, so the form encodes imagined replies, refusal, or exaggerated confidence in interpretation.
+
+# 14. Hybrid Humor
+
+Contact produces forms no source culture would have generated alone:
+
+- Atlas timing jokes performed through machine chassis movement;
+- Vast heroic poses paired with lithic slow-shadow punchlines;
+- convoy water jokes translated into oceanic pressure art;
+- clinical consent humor encoded as post-biological fork protocol;
+- children turning an alien caution pattern into a route game;
+- a corporate campaign accidentally preserving an endangered dialect phrase.
+
+Hybrid forms require traceable participation and should not always be harmonious.
+
+# 15. Anti-Homogenization Tests
+
+A valid implementation should pass blind tests where reviewers can distinguish:
+
+- a Vast boast from a corporate imitation;
+- clinical private humor from public harassment;
+- Atlas timing humor from generic technical sarcasm;
+- machine-lineage comedy from human “robot voice” writing;
+- Continuance domestic humor from Firstlight public-works humor;
+- youth mutation from an elder original;
+- an alien humor object from a randomly obscure human meme.
+
+Fail if the distinction depends only on faction names inserted into identical templates.
+
+## Atlas Maxim
+
+> **The same event may make one community laugh, another organize, another mourn, another advertise, and another fail to perceive that anything happened at all.**
